      "document_ref": "d2",
      "source_ref": "s2",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c4",
      "confidence": "low"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e6",
      "statement": "The discussion mentions that “some scholars suspect the ring may be a modern forgery, potentially connected with Émile Gilliéron fils, an art restorer and 
suspected forger who collaborated with Evans; others argue for its authenticity based on wear patterns and technical/metalworking analysis,” and that there are “stylistic similarities
with genuine Minoan art alongside suspicious aspects of the ring’s discovery story.”",
      "document_ref": "d2",
      "source_ref": "s2",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c5",
      "confidence": "low"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e7",
      "statement": "A commenter in the thread adds that “if authentic, the ring was most likely an official ‘seal’ ring used to authenticate documents,” linking the object to wider 
practices of using signet rings as markers of authority.",
      "document_ref": "d2",
      "source_ref": "s2",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c6",
      "confidence": "low"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e8",
      "statement": "The Alexandria essay describes that the 2015 excavation near the Palace of Nestor at Pylos uncovered an intact shaft grave of a Bronze Age warrior (“Griffin 
Warrior”), with grave goods including “four gold signet rings whose bezels are finely engraved with ‘Minoan ritual scenes: priestesses, bull‑jumping, religious ceremonies.’”",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c7",
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e9",
      "statement": "The blog explains that these engraved rings and the nearby agate sealstone, described as perhaps the finest miniature carving from the Aegean Bronze Age, 
demonstrate how “the ritual scenes and mythological imagery on the bezels are evidence of religious symbolism and social status; the objects were intended to carry a man’s mark long 
after his death,” framing Aegean signet rings as technologies of personal authority.",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c8",
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e10",
      "statement": "The Isopata Ring article is described as “Deconstructing a Narrative of Epiphany on the Isopata Ring,” and the snippet explains that it “critiques previous 
interpretations of the ring’s engraved scene as depicting a divine epiphany,” evaluating how earlier scholars constructed a narrative of a goddess’s epiphany from multiple female 
figures, gestures, and setting.",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c9",
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e11",
      "statement": "The article “discusses the Isopata Ring within the broader tradition of Minoan glyptic and signet rings” and “contrasts Minoan rings bearing figural scenes with 
Egyptian signet rings engraved with hieroglyphs, noting that Egyptian rings tend to have oval‑shaped bezels engraved with hieroglyphs instead of complex figural compositions, and can 
be unwieldy for wearing on the finger.”",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c10",
      "confidence": "medium"
    }
  ],
  "conflicts": []
}
