Nestor**[[a]](#cite_note-4) is a gold [signet ring](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Signet_ring \"Signet ring\") first described by the archaeologist [Arthur 
Evans](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arthur_Evans \"Arthur Evans\") in 1925. According to Evans, it was made on [Minoan Crete](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minoan_Crete \"Minoan Crete\") in the 
Late Bronze Age and discovered in a [Mycenaean tholos tomb](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tholos_tomb \"Tholos tomb\") near the ancient site of 
[Pylos](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palace_of_Nestor \"Palace of Nestor\") in [Messenia](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Messenia \"Messenia\"), in southwest Greece. The ring has a crowded and 
complex design, including human figures as well as real and mythical animals, and has been interpreted as indicating aspects of [Minoan 
religion](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minoan_religion \"Minoan religion\"). Evans considered it to show scenes from the [underworld](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Underworld \"Underworld\"), 
and to illustrate connections between Minoan belief and [ancient Egyptian religion](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Egyptian_religion \"Ancient Egyptian religion\"). It is currently 
held by the [Ashmolean Museum](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashmolean_Museum \"Ashmolean Museum\") at the [University of Oxford](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Oxford \"University 
of Oxford\"), to which Evans gifted it in 1939.\n\nSince its discovery, the ring's authenticity has been debated, with several scholars considering it a modern fake, and others 
