### 5. General scholarly background on Mycenaean Pylos, the Palace of Nestor, and Homeric traditions

These sources do not focus on the ring itself but provide solid background on Pylos, Mycenaean culture, and the Homeric Nestor context into which the ring’s traditional name fits.

#### 5a. World of the Griffin Warrior (Pylos and Homeric Nestor)

- **Title:** “World of the Griffin Warrior”  
- **URL:** https://archaeology.org/issues/september-october-2019/features/greece-pylos-mycenaean-warrior-grave  
- **Author:** Andrew Curry  
- **Publication:** *Archaeology* magazine (Archaeological Institute of America)  
- **Date:** September/October 2019  
- **Source type:** Popular but well‑researched archaeological feature  
- **Query triggering it:** "Ring of Nestor Pylos gold ring"  
- **Relevance:** Explains how Mycenaean Pylos is connected to Homeric Nestor and discusses Late Bronze Age elite graves near the Palace of Nestor (including the Griffin Warrior) in a 
way that helps frame the sort of tomb context claimed for the Ring of Nestor.

- **Content / summary:**  
  - Opens by noting that the “age of Homer” (Agamemnon, Odysseus, Nestor) has roots in a real Mycenaean past and that the Mycenaean palaces, including Pylos, provided the model for 
Homer’s heroic world. It mentions Nestor as the king of Pylos in the *Iliad* and *Odyssey* and the modern identification of the site at Ano Englianos as the Palace of Nestor.  
  - Describes the 2015 discovery of the Griffin Warrior grave near the Palace of Nestor, a rich shaft grave with weapons, jewelry, sealstones and gold rings.  
  - Emphasizes how such burials show the connection between the Mycenaean elites and Minoan Crete, with many grave goods being Minoan or Minoan‑style, reinforcing the idea that a 
luxury Minoan signet ring like the Ring of Nestor could plausibly have been buried in an elite Mycenaean tomb at Pylos.  
  - The article underscores that while Homer’s Nestor is a literary figure composed centuries later, the Mycenaean palaces and their elites provide a real archaeological substrate 
underlying those tales.

This is useful to contextualize the practice (including by Evans) of naming artifacts and palaces after Homeric figures.

#### 5b. Griffin Warrior Tomb project site (Pylos elite graves & Minoan rings, for broader Mycenaean practice)

- **Title:** “Griffin Warrior Tomb”  
- **URL:** https://griffinwarrior.org/griffin-warrior-tomb  
- **Institution:** University of Cincinnati (project website)  
- **Date:** Site content published post‑2015; text updated by project team (Stocker & Davis).  
- **Source type:** Institutional excavation project website  
- **Query triggering it:** "Ring of Nestor Pylos gold ring"  
- **Relevance:** Shows that Mycenaean elite burials at Pylos (e.g., Griffin Warrior) did in fact contain multiple Minoan gold rings. While not about the Ring of Nestor itself, it 
gives archaeological plausibility to Evans’s claim of a Minoan gold signet ring found in a Mycenaean tomb near the Palace of Nestor.

- **Content / summary:**  
  - Describes the 2015 discovery of the Griffin Warrior’s grave in a previously unexplored field “near the palace” at Pylos.  
  - The tomb was a stone‑lined shaft grave with a single adult male; the burial was intact and exceptionally wealthy.  
  - Grave goods: gold cups, hundreds of beads, a gold chain and pendant, dozens of sealstones and four gold rings, ivory plaque with a griffon, bronze weapons, bronze jugs and basin, 
boar’s tusk helmet fragments, bronze armor strips, etc.  
  - The text emphasizes that the warrior was a prominent local leader at the very beginning of Mycenaean civilization, when Minoan‑style architecture and artifacts were being adopted 
on the mainland.  
  - Crucially, it notes that the majority of the ca. 1500 objects with which he was buried are of Minoan style or Cretan manufacture.  

Use this to support general statements about (1) Mycenaean Pylos as an elite center, (2) the presence of Cretan/Minoan luxury goods, including gold signet rings, in elite tombs near 
the Palace of Nestor, which is the context Evans claimed for the Ring of Nestor.

#### 5c. Davis & Stocker, “The Lord of the Gold Rings: The Griffin Warrior of Pylos” (for Mycenaean–Minoan ring culture at Pylos)

- **Title:** “The Lord of the Gold Rings: The Griffin Warrior of Pylos”  
- **URL (abstract/open info):** https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/index.php/publications/hesperia/article/85/4/627-655  
- **Authors:** Jack L. Davis & Sharon R. Stocker  
- **Journal:** *Hesperia* 85.4 (2016), 627–655  
- **Source type:** Peer‑reviewed journal article (Aegean archaeology)  
- **Query triggering it:** "Ring of Nestor Pylos gold ring"  
- **Relevance:** Addresses gold signet rings in a Mycenaean grave near the Palace of Nestor, dating (Late Helladic IIA, ca. 1500–1450 BCE), and their Minoan iconography. While not 
about the Ring of Nestor per se, it robustly frames how Minoan rings circulate in Mycenaean elite contexts at Pylos.

- **Content / summary (from abstract and limited visible text):**  
  - Reports the discovery, excavation, and contents of the Griffin Warrior’s tomb.  
  - Dates the tomb to Late Helladic IIA (ca. 1450 BCE), just before the main period of the Pylian palace complex.  
  - Among the grave goods are four gold signet rings decorated with Minoan ritual scenes. Davis & Stocker discuss the iconography of those rings and their significance for 
understanding Mycenaean ideology, political power, and religious concepts.  
  - The article argues that the rings are Minoan in style, probably of Cretan manufacture, but they were buried with a Mycenaean warrior on the mainland, showing intensive cultural 
exchange.  
  - This directly parallels the claimed situation of the Ring of Nestor: Cretan‑made, Minoan‑style gold signet ring in a Mycenaean tomb near Pylos dated to the earlier phase of 
Mycenaean palatial culture.

