### 6. General Mycenaean funerary and cultural context (for dating and interpretation framework)

Although not specific to the ring, these help you ground dating and interpretive statements.

#### 6a. Mycenaean ring from Ialyssos (for comparative dating and typology of Mycenaean gold signet rings)

- **Title:** “Gold Mycenaean ring returned to Greece”  
- **URL:** https://www.archaeology.wiki/blog/2022/05/23/gold-mycenaean-ring-returned-to-greece  
- **Institution:** Archaeology & Arts / Greek Ministry of Culture (news report)  
- **Date:** 23 May 2022  
- **Source type:** News article with archaeological context  
- **Query triggering it:** "Ring of Nestor Mycenaean Pylos gold ring"  
- **Relevance:** Describes another Mycenaean gold signet ring (from Ialyssos, Rhodes) with clear archaeological context and a known excavation history; useful as a comparative example
of Mycenaean signet rings and burial offerings.

- **Content / summary:**  
  - Reports the return to Greece of a gold Mycenaean signet ring depicting a pair of sphinxes from Tomb 61 in the Mycenaean necropolis of Ialyssos on Rhodes.  
  - The ring was excavated in 1927 by the Italian Archaeological School and kept in the Archaeological Museum of Rhodes before being stolen during World War II. It resurfaced in the 
collection of Nobel laureate Georg von Békésy and was eventually returned to Greece in 2022.  
  - This example shows the type of iconography (mythical creatures like sphinxes) and funerary treatment (grave offering in a Mycenaean tomb) that is typical for high‑status Late 
Bronze Age signet rings, and reinforces the plausibility of the Ring of Nestor being a buried elite object.

