Nestor**[[a]](#cite_note-4) is a gold [signet ring](/wiki/Signet_ring \"Signet ring\") first described by the archaeologist [Arthur Evans](/wiki/Arthur_Evans \"Arthur Evans\") in 
1925. According to Evans, it was made on [Minoan Crete](/wiki/Minoan_Crete \"Minoan Crete\") in the Late Bronze Age and discovered in a [Mycenaean tholos tomb](/wiki/Tholos_tomb 
\"Tholos tomb\") near the ancient site of [Pylos](/wiki/Palace_of_Nestor \"Palace of Nestor\") in [Messenia](/wiki/Messenia \"Messenia\"), in southwest Greece. The ring has a crowded 
and complex design, including human figures as well as real and mythical animals, and has been interpreted as indicating aspects of [Minoan religion](/wiki/Minoan_religion \"Minoan 
religion\"). Evans considered it to show scenes from the [underworld](/wiki/Underworld \"Underworld\"), and to illustrate connections between Minoan belief and [ancient Egyptian 
religion](/wiki/Ancient_Egyptian_religion \"Ancient Egyptian religion\"). It is currently held by the [Ashmolean Museum](/wiki/Ashmolean_Museum \"Ashmolean Museum\") at the 
[University of Oxford](/wiki/University_of_Oxford \"University of Oxford\"), to which Evans gifted it in 1939.\n\nSince its discovery, the ring's authenticity has been debated, with 
several scholars considering it a modern fake, and others defending it as a genuinely ancient artefact. Arguments in both directions have been made on the grounds of iconographical 
consistency with other Minoan artworks, and based upon the potential role of [Émile Gilliéron *fils*](/wiki/%C3%89mile_Gilli%C3%A9ron_fils \"Émile Gilliéron fils\"), an art restorer, 
suspected forger, and prolific collaborator of Evans's, in the ring's discovery. Other arguments for authenticity include the wear evident upon the ring and microscopic analysis of 
