Encyclopedia](/static/images/mobile/copyright/wikipedia-tagline-en-25.svg)\n\n## Contents\n\n# Palace of Nestor\n\n[37°01′38″N 21°41′42″E﻿ / ﻿37.02722°N 21.69500°E﻿ / 37.02722; 
21.69500](https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Palace_of_Nestor&params=37_01_38_N_21_41_42_E_)\n\n![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Ancient_Bath_in
_Pylos.jpg/330px-Ancient_Bath_in_Pylos.jpg)\n\nThe **Palace of Nestor** ([Modern Greek](/wiki/Modern_Greek \"Modern Greek\"): Ανάκτορο του Νέστορα) was an important centre in 
Mycenaean times and is traditionally identified with the palace of the Homeric king [Nestor](/wiki/Nestor_(mythology) \"Nestor (mythology)\") at \"sandy [Pylos](/wiki/Pylos 
\"Pylos\")\", described in [Homer](/wiki/Homer \"Homer\")'s *[Odyssey](/wiki/Odyssey \"Odyssey\")* and *[Iliad](/wiki/Iliad \"Iliad\")*.[[1]](#cite_note-1)\n\nThe palace featured in 
the story of the [Trojan War](/wiki/Trojan_War \"Trojan War\"), as Homer tells us that [Telemachus](/wiki/Telemachus \"Telemachus\"):\n\nwent to Pylos and to Nestor, the shepherd of 
the people, and he received me in his lofty house and gave me kindly welcome, as a father might his own son who after a long time had newly come from afar: even so kindly he tended me
with his glorious sons.[[2]](#cite_note-2)\n\nThe archeological site is the best preserved [Mycenaean Greek](/wiki/Mycenaean_Greece \"Mycenaean Greece\") palace discovered. The palace
is the primary structure within a larger [Late Helladic](/wiki/Late_Helladic \"Late Helladic\") era settlement, once probably surrounded by a fortified wall. The [palace](/wiki/Palace
\"Palace\") was a two-storey building with store rooms, workshops, baths, light wells, reception rooms and a [sewage](/wiki/Sewage \"Sewage\") system.\n\nThe settlement had been long 
occupied, with most artifacts discovered dating from 1300 BC. The palace complex was destroyed by fire around 1250 BC.\n\n![On Sunday, June 12, 2016, inaugurated the upgraded 
archaeological site with new roof protection, covering single central palace monument area 3,185 sq.m. and provides the opportunity to visit the palace with hanging 
vestibules.](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/%CE%A0%CE%B1%CE%BB%CE%AC%CF%84%CE%B9_%CF%84%CE%BF%CF%85_%CE%9D%CE%AD%CF%83%CF%84%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%B1_-_%CE%9D%CE%AD%CE%B
F_%CF%83%CF%84%CE%AD%CE%B3%CE%B1%CF%83%CF%84%CF%81%CE%BF_%CF%80%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%83%CF%84%CE%B1%CF%83%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82.jpg/250px-%CE%A0%CE%B1%CE%BB%CE%AC%CF%84%CE%B9_%CF%84%CE%BF%CF%85
_%CE%9D%CE%AD%CF%83%CF%84%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%B1_-_%CE%9D%CE%AD%CE%BF_%CF%83%CF%84%CE%AD%CE%B3%CE%B1%CF%83%CF%84%CF%81%CE%BF_%CF%80%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%83%CF%84%CE%B1%CF%83%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82.jp
g)\n\nIn June 2016, the site reopened to the public after the roof was replaced by a modern structure with raised walkways for 
visitors.[[3]](#cite_note-arch-3)[[4]](#cite_note-amna-4)[[5]](#cite_note-open-5)\n\n## Location\n\nThe site is on the hill of Epano Englianos, situated close to the road 4 kilometres
(2.5 mi) south of [Chora](/wiki/Chora,_Messenia \"Chora, Messenia\") and 17 kilometres (11 mi) north of [Pylos](/wiki/Pylos \"Pylos\"), at 150 metres (490 ft) above sea level and in 
an area of 170 metres (560 ft) by 90 metres (300 ft).\n\n## 
Excavations\n\n![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Hunter_and_stag_wall_fresco_fragment.jpg/250px-Hunter_and_stag_wall_fresco_fragment.jpg)\n\nIn 1912 and 1926, 
two [tholos tombs](/wiki/Beehive_tomb \"Beehive tomb\") north of the Bay of [Navarino](/wiki/Pylos \"Pylos\") were excavated. One contained three decorated jars, and the other a 
collection of Early [Mycenaean](/wiki/Mycenaean_Greece \"Mycenaean Greece\") and [Middle Helladic](/wiki/Middle_Helladic \"Middle Helladic\") pots.\n\nA joint Hellenic-American 
expedition was formed with the [Greek Archaeological Service](/wiki/Greek_Archaeological_Service \"Greek Archaeological Service\") and the [University of Cincinnat…(truncated)"}, 
