[tools:69ee6827-8005-0ce9-a1ff-bf90e9922f92] [AIMessage → tool] submit_final_report({'title': 'Ring of Nestor', 'sections': [{'title': 'Introduction', 'blocks': [{'text': 'The Ring of
Nestor is a gold signet ring attributed to the Aegean Bronze Age and notable for its elaborate engraved imagery and its contested role within Minoan and Mycenaean religious and 
artistic traditions.[1][2][9][11] Modern scholarship has focused on the ring’s complex iconography, its proposed Late Minoan dating, and ongoing debates about its authenticity and 
archaeological provenance.[1][2][9][11]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161159720689_9a6cfa4e', '20260703161159851878_180c3d65', '20260703161436959810_fbeeb753', 
'20260703161437060334_de26158a'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Discovery, Dating, and Physical Description', 'blocks': [{'text': 'Arthur Evans’s 1925 study describes a gold 
signet ring he termed the "Ring of Nestor" as part of what he called a "veritable royal treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals" from a Mycenaean rock-cut tomb near Thisbê in 
Boeotia.[9][10] Evans presents the ring as one of several finely made gold signet rings, associating the assemblage with high-status or royal burial and emphasizing the exceptional 
wealth represented by the treasure.[9][10]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161436959810_fbeeb753', '20260703161437015865_3bcb7157'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'In Evans’s 
reconstruction, the tomb group and its grave goods are dated to the late Mycenaean period, correlated with the Cretan Late Minoan III timeframe.[9] He situates the sepulchral 
treasure, including the Ring of Nestor, within this late phase of the Bronze Age, drawing on associated weapons, glass, and ceramic finds to support the chronological placement.[9]', 
'source_ids': ['20260703161436959810_fbeeb753'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'A later online encyclopedia entry reports a different discovery context, stating that the Ring of 
Nestor is a gold signet ring discovered before 1907 at Kakovatos in Messenia, Greece.[11] The same entry identifies the present location of the ring as the Ashmolean Museum at the 
University of Oxford, under inventory number AN1938.1130 and catalogued in the Corpus der Minoischen und Mykenischen Siegel as CMS VI.229.[11] It notes that Evans donated the ring to 
the Ashmolean Museum in 1939.[11]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161437060334_de26158a'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The online encyclopedia’s information box lists the ring’s 
material simply as gold and provides dimensions and weight, emphasizing that it is a large gold signet ring.[11] While these measurements are presented as part of basic object data, 
they are reported without detailed discussion of manufacturing techniques in the summarized sources and thus have not been independently contextualized in the available scholarship 
cited here.[11]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161437060334_de26158a'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The same online encyclopedia places the Ring of Nestor in the Late Minoan I period, 
approximately 1700–1450 BCE, and assigns it to Minoan culture, explicitly noting that this cultural attribution is disputed.[11] By contrast, Evans’s original publication dates the 
sepulchral treasure, including the ring, to a late Mycenaean horizon corresponding to Late Minoan III, underlining a chronological shift between the early interpretation and later 
summaries.[9][11]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161436959810_fbeeb753', '20260703161437060334_de26158a'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Theodore Eliopoulos’s 2013 article treats the 
Ring of Nestor as an object proposed to be of Late Minoan I date and Cretan origin and examines this attribution within the wider discussion of Aegean forgeries.[1] Eliopoulos’s 
analysis takes the suggested Late Minoan date into account while evaluating whether the ring’s iconography and stylistic features genuinely fit within the Late Minoan artistic 
repertoire or instead indicate modern manufacture.[1]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161159720689_9a6cfa4e'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'A social-media discussion summarizing popular 
knowledge about the Ring of Nestor describes it as a roughly 3,700-year-old gold signet ring from Crete, dating to the Late Minoan period (c. 1700–1450 BCE), and states that it is 
housed in the Ashmolean Museum in Oxford.[2] This summary reiterates Evans’s early association with Minoan Crete while aligning the object with a Late Minoan I timeframe and 
emphasizing ongoing doubts about its authenticity.[2]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161159851878_180c3d65'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Archaeological and Historical Context', 
'blocks': [{'text': 'Evans’s original publication associates the Ring of Nestor with a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals from what he describes as a Mycenaean 
rock tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia.[9] In this framework, the ring belongs to a burial assemblage characterized by weapons, glass, and pottery that collectively support a late Mycenaean
date and a funerary context reflecting elite status.[9] Evans emphasizes the tomb’s role in illuminating aspects of Bronze Age funerary practice and elite material culture.[9]', 
'source_ids': ['20260703161436959810_fbeeb753'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'In contrast, the online encyclopedia entry places the discovery of the Ring of Nestor before 1907 at 
Kakovatos in Messenia, linking it to Mycenaean tholos tombs in that region and, in Evans’s view as reported there, to a burial near Pylos.[11] The entry states that Evans argued the 
ring was made in Minoan Crete during the Late Bronze Age and came from a Mycenaean tholos tomb near Pylos, thus situating the object within the funerary landscape of southwestern 
Peloponnese rather than Boeotia.[11]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161437060334_de26158a'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'These differing accounts underscore a conflict in the reported 
discovery location: Evans’s own description contextualizes the ring within a Mycenaean rock-cut tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia, whereas the later online summary situates it in Kakovatos,
Messenia, and connects it to a tomb near Pylos.[9][11] The available sources do not resolve this discrepancy, and modern iconographic studies such as Eliopoulos’s article focus 
primarily on stylistic and authenticity questions rather than re-documenting the findspot.[1][9][11]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161436959810_fbeeb753', '20260703161437060334_de26158a',
'20260703161159720689_9a6cfa4e'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The broader archaeological setting of Pylos is presented in modern scholarship as the center of a Mycenaean kingdom 
traditionally associated with the Homeric figure Nestor.[12][13] Exhibitions such as "The Pylos of Nestor. A Mycenaean Kingdom Revealed" trace the development from local centers of 
power to the palace at Ano Englianos, where the kingdom "traditionally associated with the Homeric Nestor" took shape, emphasizing burial monuments with impressive grave goods as 
evidence of elite display and social hierarchy.[12]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161941120705_4b8208ce', '20260703161941158286_79a55ec8'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Archaeological 
syntheses describe the palace at Ano Englianos—often called the "Palace of Nestor"—as comprising four main units (Main Building, Southwest Building, Workshop, and Wine Magazine) built
with stone rubble walls, ashlar facades, mudbrick upper structures, wooden elements, plastered walls, and concrete floors.[13] Excavations beginning in 1939 and major campaigns under 
Carl Blegen between 1952 and 1964 revealed that a Middle Helladic town on the hill was cleared to make way for a Late Helladic IIIA palace, with most surviving architecture dated to 
Late Helladic IIIB.[13]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161941158286_79a55ec8'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The palace at Pylos lacks the massive cyclopean fortifications seen at 
Mycenae and Tiryns, though a gateway at the northeast end of the hill and wall fragments indicate some degree of fortification.[13] This architectural context frames the environment 
in which Mycenaean elites, possibly including those associated with elaborate signet rings, operated, providing a backdrop for understanding high-status artifacts linked to Pylian 
funerary and administrative practices.[12][13]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161941158286_79a55ec8', '20260703161941120705_4b8208ce'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The exhibition on 
Pylos highlights interconnections between Messenia and the Minoan world, the wider Mycenaean sphere, and the Eastern Mediterranean.[12] It showcases gold and bronze grave goods from 
tholos tombs at Myrsinochori and Peristeria and a gold-capped agate seal stone with a combat scene from the Grave of the Griffin Warrior near Pylos, demonstrating how funerary 
assemblages with precious metal rings and seal stones reflect cross-cultural contacts and elite identities.[7][12]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161206962184_c7b3fb03', 
'20260703161941120705_4b8208ce'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Within this broader context, the Griffin Warrior shaft grave near the Palace of Nestor yielded four gold signet rings
decorated with Minoan ritual scenes, as well as a finely engraved agate sealstone.[5][6][7] These finds have been used to illustrate how Mycenaean elites appropriated and displayed 
Minoan religious iconography, suggesting that gold signet rings in Pylian burials could function as markers of authority and as vehicles for imported or hybrid religious 
symbolism.[5][6][7]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161206795739_3a735c31', '20260703161206910482_111f8d3a', '20260703161206962184_c7b3fb03'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Evidence from 
faunal remains and burn patterns at the Palace of Nestor indicates that burnt animal sacrifice of bare, defleshed bones—a ritual practice described in Homer’s Odyssey and documented 
in later Greek archaeology—is clearly attested in the Late Bronze Age context of Pylos.[14] Scholars have interpreted this as suggesting continuity or antecedents of Homeric-style 
sacrificial practices at the palace, strengthening the link between the archaeological site and Homeric religious traditions.[14]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161941193848_a4b664b7'], 
'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Artistic Features and Iconography', 'blocks': [{'text': 'Evans’s 1925 discussion emphasizes the Ring of Nestor as a finely made gold signet ring 
with a complex figural composition engraved on its bezel.[9] He interprets the imagery as providing a glimpse into a "Minoan after-world" and as reflecting religious conceptions 
concerning the afterlife and the underworld, which he compares with ancient Egyptian beliefs.[9] The ring is described as part of a "royal treasure" whose craftsmanship and elaborate 
iconography underscore its association with high-ranking individuals.[9]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161436959810_fbeeb753'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The online encyclopedia 
summarizes the ring’s imagery as featuring human figures along with both real and fantastical animals and notes that these have been read as depicting scenes from Minoan religion, 
specifically an underworld or afterlife setting.[11] The entry also reports that Evans saw connections between the ring’s iconography and Egyptian religious imagery, highlighting 
comparative approaches that situate the ring within wider eastern Mediterranean iconographic traditions.[9][11]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161437060334_de26158a', 
'20260703161436959810_fbeeb753'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Theodore Eliopoulos’s article offers a detailed iconographic analysis of the engraved scene on the Ring of Nestor, 
examining the figures, their attributes, and the setting to assess possible narrative or ritual meaning.[1] Eliopoulos situates the ring’s imagery within Minoan and Mycenaean 
religious and artistic traditions, comparing its human and animal figures to Aegean glyptic art in order to evaluate whether the composition fits Late Minoan styles or appears as a 
modern pastiche.[1]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161159720689_9a6cfa4e'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Eliopoulos re-examines earlier interpretations, including Evans’s view of the 
ring as depicting Minoan religious iconography, in light of modern debates about Aegean forgeries.[1] By scrutinizing iconographic details and stylistic parallels, the study considers
whether certain features might reflect knowledge of Minoan art available in the early twentieth century rather than genuine Bronze Age artistic conventions, thereby using iconography 
as a tool for assessing authenticity.[1]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161159720689_9a6cfa4e'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'A social-media discussion summarizing the ring’s features 
describes the Ring of Nestor as a Late Minoan gold signet ring from Crete whose engraved imagery was interpreted by Evans as depicting religious scenes possibly associated with the 
underworld.[2] The post further reports that Evans suggested links between Minoan civilization and ancient Egypt based on the ring’s iconography, aligning this interpretation with 
broader comparative studies of Aegean and Egyptian religious art.[2]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161159851878_180c3d65'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Comparative work on Aegean 
signet rings, such as studies of the Isopata Ring, highlights the methodological difficulty of inferring complete myth narratives from Minoan figural compositions.[4] One article 
critiques earlier readings of the Isopata Ring scene as a detailed narrative of a goddess’s epiphany and instead stresses that complex arrangements of female figures, gestures, and 
settings on Minoan rings cannot be straightforwardly translated into specific mythological stories.[4] This cautionary approach informs modern evaluations of the Ring of Nestor’s 
imagery, encouraging restraint in constructing elaborate narratives from its figural program.[1][4]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161159951369_2cc3a441', '20260703161159720689_9a6cfa4e'],
'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The same Isopata Ring study contrasts Minoan signet rings, which often bear complex figural scenes, with Egyptian signet rings that typically feature 
oval bezels engraved with hieroglyphs and may be unwieldy as finger rings.[4] This comparison underscores differences in glyptic traditions and suggests that Minoan rings like the 
Ring of Nestor belong to an Aegean practice of using small, portable objects to carry intricate visual compositions rich in religious or symbolic meaning, rather than functioning 
primarily as practical sealing devices.[1][4]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161159951369_2cc3a441', '20260703161159720689_9a6cfa4e'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Scholarly Debates 
and Significance', 'blocks': [{'text': 'From Evans’s perspective, the Ring of Nestor was significant as an insight into Minoan conceptions of the afterlife and the underworld, 
offering what he termed a glimpse into the "Minoan after-world".[9] He viewed the ring as a key piece of evidence for reconstructing Bronze Age religious beliefs and for comparing 
Minoan and Egyptian conceptions of the afterlife, thus granting it considerable interpretive weight in early twentieth-century scholarship on Aegean religion.[9]', 'source_ids': 
['20260703161436959810_fbeeb753'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Later discussions, including the online encyclopedia entry, highlight that the ring’s cultural attribution to Minoan
Crete and its suggested Late Minoan I date are disputed.[11] The entry notes that some modern scholars regard the ring as a possible modern forgery, while others argue for its 
authenticity using evidence such as wear patterns, microscopic examination, and iconographic comparison.[11] This debate frames the Ring of Nestor as a contested artifact whose 
significance for understanding Bronze Age religion and art depends on its status as either genuine or forged.[1][2][11]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161437060334_de26158a', 
'20260703161159720689_9a6cfa4e', '20260703161159851878_180c3d65'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Eliopoulos’s 2013 article situates the Ring of Nestor explicitly within the context 
of Aegean forgeries and authenticity debates.[1] The study compares the ring’s human and animal figures to Minoan and Mycenaean seal imagery to judge whether its iconography matches 
Late Minoan art or suggests a modern pastiche.[1] Eliopoulos considers a proposed Late Minoan I date and Cretan origin but also entertains the possibility that the ring is a modern 
creation linked to early twentieth-century Aegean restorers such as Émile Gilliéron.[1]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161159720689_9a6cfa4e'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'A 
social-media discussion echoes these scholarly concerns, reporting that some scholars suspect the Ring of Nestor may be a modern forgery potentially connected with Émile Gilliéron 
fils, an art restorer and suspected forger who collaborated with Evans.[2] Other participants in the discussion argue for the ring’s authenticity based on wear patterns, technical and
metalworking analysis, and stylistic similarities with genuine Minoan art, while also noting suspicious aspects of the ring’s discovery story.[2] This illustrates the range of opinion
on the ring’s status and underscores that its significance is debated.[2]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161159851878_180c3d65'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Beyond authenticity, 
broader studies of Aegean signet rings use objects like the Ring of Nestor and the Griffin Warrior rings to explore how engraved rings functioned as technologies of personal authority
in the Bronze Age.[3][5][6] Analyses of the Griffin Warrior grave describe four gold signet rings engraved with Minoan ritual scenes and emphasize that their ritual imagery and fine 
craftsmanship conveyed religious symbolism and social status, with the rings intended to carry a man’s mark long after his death.[3][5][6] This framework suggests that, if genuine, 
the Ring of Nestor would have participated in similar practices of encoding identity and authority into portable, high-status artifacts.[2][3]', 'source_ids': 
['20260703161159902493_4a2a7ffc', '20260703161206795739_3a735c31', '20260703161206910482_111f8d3a', '20260703161159851878_180c3d65'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'One commenter in 
the social-media discussion explicitly suggests that, if authentic, the Ring of Nestor most likely functioned as an official seal ring used to authenticate documents, consistent with 
broader practices of using signet rings as markers of authority.[2] While this is a modern inference rather than a direct ancient testimony, it aligns with scholarship that interprets
Aegean signet rings as combining practical sealing functions with the display of religious and social symbolism.[2][3]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161159851878_180c3d65', 
'20260703161159902493_4a2a7ffc'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Comparative iconographic studies, such as the critique of narrative reconstructions on the Isopata Ring, have 
influenced scholarly treatment of the Ring of Nestor by cautioning against building detailed mythological narratives from glyptic scenes.[4][1] By emphasizing the limits of 
iconographic evidence and the risk of over-interpretation, such work encourages a more restrained and methodologically rigorous assessment of what the Ring of Nestor can reveal about 
Bronze Age religious ideas, even if it is accepted as authentic.[1][4]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161159951369_2cc3a441', '20260703161159720689_9a6cfa4e'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, 
{'title': 'Provenance, Subsequent History, and Museum Curation', 'blocks': [{'text': 'Evans’s 1925 publication treats the Ring of Nestor and its associated gold signet rings and bead 
seals as a sepulchral treasure from a Mycenaean rock tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia.[9][10] In this account, the ring’s early provenance is tied to this burial context and to Evans’s 
broader interest in linking Mycenaean tomb assemblages with Minoan religious imagery, although the details of acquisition and transfer to his collection are not specified in the 
summarized sources.[9][10]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161436959810_fbeeb753', '20260703161437015865_3bcb7157'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The online encyclopedia reports that the
Ring of Nestor was discovered before 1907 at Kakovatos in Messenia and later entered the Ashmolean Museum’s collection.[11] According to this source, Evans donated the ring to the 
Ashmolean Museum in 1939, and it is now held there under inventory number AN1938.1130, with the Corpus der Minoischen und Mykenischen Siegel referencing it as CMS VI.229.[11] This 
establishes a chain of custody from discovery (as reported) through Evans’s possession to its eventual status as a museum object in Oxford.[11]', 'source_ids': 
['20260703161437060334_de26158a'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The same encyclopedia notes that the ring’s reported findspot and Evans’s role in its collection history have been 
scrutinized in modern debates about authenticity, with some scholars raising the possibility that restorers such as Émile Gilliéron fils may have been involved in its 
manufacture.[11][1][2] Because the summarized sources do not provide independent archival documentation of the ring’s excavation or acquisition, questions about its provenance remain 
part of the broader authenticity discussion rather than fully resolved historical facts.[1][2][11]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161437060334_de26158a', '20260703161159720689_9a6cfa4e', 
'20260703161159851878_180c3d65'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'While detailed exhibition histories of the Ring of Nestor are not outlined in the available sources, broader museum 
and exhibition narratives concerning Pylos and Mycenaean Messenia provide a relevant curatorial context.[8][12] The exhibition "The Pylos of Nestor. A Mycenaean Kingdom Revealed," 
organized by the Greek Ministry of Culture and Sports, the Ephorate of Antiquities of Messenia, the J. Paul Getty Museum, and the University of Cincinnati, displays 214 objects from 
the Archaeological Museum of Messenia and 11 from the National Archaeological Museum to illustrate the development of the kingdom associated with Nestor and its burial monuments with 
impressive grave goods.[8][12]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161207013830_6036de22', '20260703161941120705_4b8208ce'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'This traveling exhibition has been 
shown in Kalamata, at the J. Paul Getty Museum, and at the National Archaeological Museum in Athens, highlighting connections between Messenia, the Minoan world, the wider Mycenaean 
sphere, and the Eastern Mediterranean.[8][12] Although the Ring of Nestor itself is not explicitly listed among the exhibited objects in the summarized material, the exhibition’s 
focus on gold rings, seal stones, and other elite grave goods from Pylos-related contexts illustrates how such artifacts are curated as key evidence for Mycenaean kingship, religion, 
and cross-cultural contacts.[5][7][8][12]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161207013830_6036de22', '20260703161941120705_4b8208ce', '20260703161206795739_3a735c31', 
'20260703161206962184_c7b3fb03'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Name and Association with Homeric Traditions', 'blocks': [{'text': 'Evans’s choice of the name "Ring of Nestor" 
reflects an implicit association with the Homeric figure Nestor, whose palace at Pylos is a central setting in the Odyssey.[9][13] Modern archaeological narratives and public-oriented
syntheses refer to the Mycenaean palace at Ano Englianos as "Nestor’s Palace," explicitly identifying it as the home of Homer’s hero Nestor.[13] Exhibitions and articles thus frame 
Pylos as a kingdom traditionally associated with Nestor, using this Homeric link to structure interpretations of Mycenaean political and religious life.[12][13]', 'source_ids': 
['20260703161436959810_fbeeb753', '20260703161941158286_79a55ec8', '20260703161941120705_4b8208ce'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Scholarly work on burnt animal sacrifice at the 
Palace of Nestor suggests that ritual practices described in Homer’s Odyssey, such as burnt sacrifice of defleshed bones, have clear antecedents in Late Bronze Age Pylos.[14] This 
evidence has been taken to indicate continuity between Mycenaean religious rituals and later Homeric descriptions, reinforcing the idea that objects linked to Pylos’s 
elite—potentially including high-status rings and seals—exist within a ritual landscape connected to Homeric traditions.[14]', 'source_ids': ['20260703161941193848_a4b664b7'], 'kind':
'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'References', 'blocks': [{'text': '[1] Theodore Eliopoulos, "SOME OBSERVATIONS ON THE ICONOGRAPHY OF THE ‘RING OF NESTOR’", Annuario della Scuola 
Archeologica di Atene e delle Missioni Italiane in Oriente XCI (2013), pp. 209–228. Available at: 
https://www.academia.edu/83658257/SOME_OBSERVATIONS_ON_THE_ICONOGRAPHY_OF_THE_RING_OF_NESTOR_', 'source_ids': ['20260703161159720689_9a6cfa4e'], 'kind': 'list_item'}, {'text': '[2] 
"Ring of Nestor - A ~3700-year-old gold signet ring from Crete, Late Minoan Period" (Facebook post in group "Archaeology & Civilizations"). Available at: 
https://www.facebook.com/groups/archeologyandcivilizations/posts/26471243725875801', 'source_ids': ['20260703161159851878_180c3d65'], 'kind': 'list_item'}, {'text': '[3] "The Signet 
Ring: A History of Authority Worn on the Hand", Alexandria. Available at: https://alexandria.co/blogs/alexandria/signet-ring', 'source_ids': ['20260703161159902493_4a2a7ffc'], 'kind':
'list_item'}, {'text': '[4] "Deconstructing a Narrative of Epiphany on the Isopata Ring", Journal of Near Eastern Studies. Available at: 
https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/pdfplus/10.2307/507325', 'source_ids': ['20260703161159951369_2cc3a441'], 'kind': 'list_item'}, {'text': '[5] "The Lord of the Gold Rings", 
Academia.edu (paper on the Griffin Warrior grave at Pylos). Available at: https://www.academia.edu/116392582/The_Lord_of_the_Gold_Rings', 'source_ids': 
['20260703161206795739_3a735c31'], 'kind': 'list_item'}, {'text': '[6] Jack L. Davis and colleagues, "The Lord of the Gold Rings: The Griffin Warrior of Pylos", ResearchGate. 
Available at: https://www.researchgate.net/publication/310467426_The_Lord_of_the_Gold_Rings_The_Griffin_Warrior_of_Pylos', 'source_ids': ['20260703161206910482_111f8d3a'], 'kind': 
